                              PREAMBLE

  The GNU Affero General Public License is a free, copyleft license for software
  and other kinds of works, specifically designed to ensure cooperation with the
  community in the case of network server software.

  When we speak of free software, we are referring to freedom, not price. Our
  General Public Licenses are designed to make sure that you have the freedom to
  distribute copies of free software (and charge for them if you wish), that you
  receive source code or can get it if you want it, that you can change the
  software or use pieces of it in new free programs, and that you know you can do
  these things.

  Developers that use the GNU GPL protect your rights with two steps: (1) assert
  copyright on the software, and (2) offer you this License which gives you legal
  permission to copy, distribute and/or modify the software.

  A key problem facing network-server software is that the GPL's copyleft does
  not require operators of such servers to release their changes. The AGPL closes
  this gap: if you run a modified version of the Program on a server and let
  users interact with it there, you must also make that modified source available.

  The precise terms and conditions for copying, distribution and modification
  follow.

